Name: setWakeWord

Type: command

Syntax: setWakeWord <word> [, <timeoutMS>]

Summary:
Sets a wake word (or phrase) that must be spoken before the engine will
match registered voice commands. Pass empty to clear the wake word and
return to always-on command matching.

Introduced: 1.1.0

OS: mac

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- Require "hey hyper" before any command is accepted
setWakeWord "hey hyper"

Example:
-- Wake word with a 10-second command window
setWakeWord "hey hyper", 10000

Example:
-- Clear the wake word so commands fire without a trigger phrase
setWakeWord ""

Example:
-- Full setup with wake word
setWakeWord "computer", 8000
registerVoiceCommand "open stack"
registerVoiceCommand "save stack"
startListening

on wakeWordDetected
    -- Optional: give the user audio/visual feedback that we're listening
    beep
end wakeWordDetected

on voiceCommand pPhrase
    if pPhrase is "open stack" then doMenu "Open Stack..."
    if pPhrase is "save stack" then save this stack
end voiceCommand

on listenTimeoutExpired
    -- The command window closed with no match
    play "timeout.aiff"
end listenTimeoutExpired

Parameters:
word (string): The wake word or wake phrase. Case-insensitive. Pass
empty to clear the wake word and return to always-on command matching.

timeoutMS (integer): Optional. The number of milliseconds the engine
waits for a registered command after the wake word is detected. Defaults
to 5000 (5 seconds). Values less than or equal to zero are ignored and
the default is used.

Description:
When a wake word is set the engine operates in two modes:

1. Wake-word mode (waiting) — only the wake word phrase is matched.
   All other spoken audio is ignored.

2. Command window (active) — entered after the wake word is detected
   and a <wakeWordDetected> message is dispatched. During this window,
   registered voice command phrases are matched normally. If a command
   is recognised, a <voiceCommand> message fires and the engine returns
   to wake-word mode. If no command is recognised within <timeoutMS>
   milliseconds, a <listenTimeoutExpired> message fires and the engine
   returns to wake-word mode.

When no wake word is set (the default, or after passing empty), every
recognised command fires <voiceCommand> directly without a trigger phrase.

Changing the wake word while the engine is already listening takes effect
immediately. Any active command window is cancelled.

References: startListening (command), registerVoiceCommand (command),
wakeWordDetected (message), listenTimeoutExpired (message), voiceCommand (message)

Tags: speech, voice
